 + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "260"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "274"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "288"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "302"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "316"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "330"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "340"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "354"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "368"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "382"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "396"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "410"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "424"} "1994"] "\n" [:option {:value "438"} "1998"] "\n" [:option {:value "450"} "2002"] "\n" [:option {:value "467"} "2006"] "\n" [:option {:value "924247"} "2010"] "\n" [:option {:value "350040"} "2014"] "\n" [:option {:value "15000087"} "2026"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/141"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Four, Men" [:small {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "The 4-man event was originally scheduled for 11-12 February, but was postponed by a snowstorm, which necessitated this event being completed after the Closing Ceremony.", :data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right"} "1"]]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "14 – 15 February 1932"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/74"} "Mt. Van Hoevenberg Recreation Area, Lake Placid"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/246"} "28 from 5 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Four runs, total time determined placement."]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Curves: 26" [:br {}] "Length: 2366 m" [:br {}] "Start Altitude: 850 m" [:br {}] "Vertical Drop: 228 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "As in the " [:a {:href "/results/239"} "two-man event"] ", a local team was favored for the victory. The “Red Devils” from Saranac Lake were captained by " [:a {:href "/athletes/86755"} "Henry Homburger"] ", and featured " [:a {:href "/athletes/97839"} "Paul Stevens"] ". His brothers, " [:a {:href "/athletes/97841"} "Hubert"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/97837"} "Curtis"] ", won the gold medal in the two-man event. The second U.S. bob was captained by the defending Olympic Champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/85736"} "Billy Fiske"] ". His crew consisted of " [:a {:href "/athletes/95948"} "J.J. O’Brien"] ", silver medalist in " [:a {:href "/results/236"} "1928"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/8658"} "Eddie Eagan"] ", Olympic " [:a {:href "/results/21412"} "boxing champion in 1920"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/86222"} "Clifford Gray"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Foreign opposition was expected from the German crews, but their training runs proved disastrous. Germany I, piloted by World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/1006155"} "Werner Zahn"] ", crashed out of the treacherous Zig Zag corner on 31 January, flying 50 metres through the air. Three of the crewmen were injured, and the bob was withdrawn. Two days later, the new Germany I, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/86197"} "Fritz Grau"] ", also took off, landing in a trackside tree, leaving the crew severely injured. This left " [:a {:href "/athletes/87930"} "Hanns Kilian"] "’s sled, while the second sled was filled with Germans living in the United States - only their driver, champagne seller  " [:a {:href "/athletes/98716"} "Walther von Mumm"] ", had prior bobsledding experience. The Belgians were eliminated too in the training runs, with the sled of " [:a {:href "/athletes/65215"} "Max Houben"] " capsizing, resulting in a dislocated shoulder and a damaged sled. Meanwhile, the American sleds were mysteriously absent, fiddling with their new sled runners."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Because of a thaw, the event start had to be postponed until 14 February, and it was in fact held after the closing ceremony. The delayed competition was planned to be completed in a single day, but the competitors protested and the third and fourth runs were held on the 15th. The first three runs were all won by Fiske’s crew, creating a four second gap for the final run. The Red Devils took back some time in the final descent, but not enough to keep Fiske from his second consecutive title. Eagan’s performance, a gold medal in both the Summer and Winter Olympics, has not yet been equaled by any Olympian, as of 2024. Fiske overcame a natural advantage held by Homburger, whose construction firm, Smith, Golder, &amp; Homburger, of Saranac Lake, had been enlisted by Stanislaus Zentzytzki, and Homburger consulted on the final design of the run, although it was eventually built by the firm of Leo A. Malone, of Lake Placid."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {} "Run #3"] [:th {} "Run #4"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "United States 1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} "7:53.68"] [:td {} "2:00.52 (1)"] [:td {} "1:59.16 (1)"] [:td {} "1:57.41 (1)"] [:td {} "1:56.59 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "13"} [:a {:href "/athletes/85736"} "Billy Fiske"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/8658"} "Eddie Eagan"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/86222"} "Clifford Gray"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/95948"} "J. J. O'Brien"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} "United States 2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: mid